zo le cu gadri zo cmene lu le cmene cu lojbo li'u la lojban ...

is the way I conceived of gadri being used.  I think that gadma'o is
useful for an article/descriptor taken out of context.

I presume that if I knew any French, I could come up with a sentence using
la fasyban. that would have the same x1.

As to which meaning is the gismu and whichneeds lujvo - it merely depends
on how much fiddling you want to do to get the desired place structure.
It seemed to me that any word category implicitly requires a language, since
"le" is NOT a gadri in la gliban.

